Subject: A compiled function can ask whether a constructor's fields are replayable from the engine's caches

What this does
  - `qjs_aot_ctor_replayable(ctx, ctor, sites, n, cache)` answers 1 when `new ctor(...)` is described by the
    engine's own add-transition cells: a fresh `this` has the shape the cell of `sites[0]` starts from,
    the cell of `sites[k]` leads to the shape the next one starts from, and the cells were filled at
    the current prototype-interceptor epoch (`misses == rt->ic_watchpoint_epoch`). It reads only. A caller-owned
    `JSAotCtorCache` remembers the last positive answer, keyed on the function object's shape, the object in
    its `prototype` slot, and `aot_pic_epoch`, so a repeated question is a few loads.

Why we need it
  A compiled function that proves an object made by a plain constructor never escapes does not make it:
  its fields are the constructor's arguments, and an exit builds the object then. That is unobservable
  only if running the constructor would have taken the cached add path for every field (no setter,
  read-only slot, proxy or non-extensible target on the prototype chain), which is the condition
  `regvm_put_field_add_hit` replays under. `ctor.prototype` is read as `js_create_from_ctor_cap` reads it, and the instance shape it selects is
  the one cached for that prototype, so a changed prototype with no cached instance shape answers 0.
  `ic_watchpoint_bump` (a prototype interceptor appearing) now also bumps `JSRuntime.aot_pic_epoch`, which is what a
  cached verdict about add-transition entries depends on.

